<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/04:_Organic_Compounds_-_Cycloalkanes_and_their_Stereochemistry/4.05:_Conformations_of_Cyclohexane" class="mt-section" id="section_1"><span id="Conformations_of_Cyclohexane"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-31412" id="Conformations_of_Cyclohexane-31412">Conformations of Cyclohexane</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-31412">A planar structure for cyclohexane is clearly improbable. The bond angles would necessarily be 120&ordm;, 10.5&ordm; larger than the ideal tetrahedral angle. Also, every carbon-hydrogen bond in such a structure would be eclipsed. The resulting angle and eclipsing strains would severely destabilize this structure. The ring strain of planar cyclohexane is in excess of 84 kJ/mol so it rarely discussed other than in theory.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-31412"><img alt="Molecular structure of cyclohexane. " class="default" src="data:image/png;base64," /></p>



<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-31412"><strong>Cyclohexane in the strained planar configuration showing how the hydrogens become eclipsed.</strong></p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/04:_Organic_Compounds_-_Cycloalkanes_and_their_Stereochemistry/4.05:_Conformations_of_Cyclohexane" class="mt-section" id="section_2"><span id="Chair_Conformation_of_Cyclohexane"></span><h4 class="lt-chem-31412" id="Chair_Conformation_of_Cyclohexane-31412">Chair Conformation of Cyclohexane</h4>

<p class="lt-chem-31412">The flexibility of cyclohexane allows for a conformation which is almost free of ring strain. If two carbon atoms on opposite sides of the six-membered ring are bent out of the plane of the ring, a shape is formed that resembles a reclining beach chair.  This <strong>chair conformation</strong> is the lowest energy conformation for cyclohexane with an overall ring strain of 0 kJ/mol.  In this conformation, the carbon-carbon ring bonds are able to assume bonding angles of ~111<sup>o</sup><sup> </sup>which is very near the optimal tetrahedral 109.5<sup>o</sup> so angle strain has been eliminated.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-31412"><img alt="Two different chair conformations of cyclohexane. " style="width: 271px; height: 93px;" width="271px" height="93px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/378540/two_depictions_of_the_chair_conformation_of_cyclohexane.svg?revision=2&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=271&amp;height=93" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-31412">Also, the C-H ring bonds are staggered so torsional strain has also been eliminated.  This is clearly seen when looking at a Newman projection of chair cyclohexane sighted down the two central C-C bonds.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-31412"><img style="width: 115px; height: 77px;" width="115px" height="77px" class="internal" alt="Newman projection of cyclohexane.svg" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/378545/Newman_projection_of_cyclohexane.svg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=115&amp;height=77" /></p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-31412">Newman projection of cyclohexane</p>

